# What never enters the build context.
#
# Written before the Dockerfile deliberately. The context is read on the first
# build, and the `.env` rule below is the only irreversible mistake available
# here: pre-commit refuses to commit `.env` to git, but nothing guards a build
# context, and a key baked into an image layer survives deleting the file.

# --- Credentials -----------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.local
.env.*
!.env.sample

# --- Host-specific Python --------------------------------------------------
# The image builds its own venv from uv.lock. This one holds absolute host paths
# and is also the largest thing in the context by far, so excluding it is what
# makes a rebuild fast.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/

# --- Tool caches -----------------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# --- Version control -------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing in the build reads history.
.git/
.gitignore
.pre-commit-config.yaml

# --- Generated output ------------------------------------------------------
# Gitignored and disposable. Also: every run directory holds a real child's
# name, which is personal data about a minor. It must not be copied into an
# image that could be pushed to a registry.
outputs/

# --- Real persistence ------------------------------------------------------
# Gitignored, and deliberately *mounted* rather than copied -- see the volumes
# in docker-compose.yml. Baking a knowledge index into the image would make it
# stale the moment the corpus changed, with nothing to reveal it.
data/

# --- Not needed at runtime -------------------------------------------------
docs/
Makefile

# README.md and LICENSE are deliberately NOT excluded, despite being unused at
# runtime. `pyproject.toml` references both -- `readme = "README.md"` and
# `license-files = ["LICENSE"]` -- so `uv sync` fails to build the project
# without them. The first two builds of this image failed on exactly this, once
# per file. The rule to carry forward: exclusions here must be judged against
# what the *build* reads, not only what the runtime needs.

# The committed corpus is an *ingestion* input, and ingestion is an offline step
# run on the host. Excluding it means the container reads a pre-built index from
# the mounted data/ volume and cannot re-ingest. That is the intended split for
# now; item 9 of the infrastructure spec moves the index into Postgres and
# retires the question.
corpus/

# Tests are not shipped. `make ci-local` runs on the host and in CI, not in the
# runtime image -- an image that can run its own tests needs the dev group
# installed, which is a larger image for no runtime benefit.
tests/
